Here’s a clear explanation of how Docker containers are built and run, suitable for an interview:

1. Write a Dockerfile

A Dockerfile defines how your microservice will be containerized. For a Spring Boot service, it typically looks like this:

# Use an OpenJDK base image
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-alpine

# Set working directory inside the container
WORKDIR /app

# Copy the Spring Boot JAR file into the container
COPY target/my-service.jar app.jar

# Run the JAR file
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

2. Build the Docker Image

Use the Docker CLI to build an image from the Dockerfile:

docker build -t my-service:1.0 .


-t my-service:1.0 tags the image with a name and version.

3. Run the Docker Container

Run the container locally to test it:

docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name my-service-container my-service:1.0


Explanation of flags:

-d → runs in detached mode (background).

-p 8080:8080 → maps host port 8080 to container port 8080.

--name → assigns a container name.

4. Verify the Container
docker ps            # Lists running containers
docker logs my-service-container  # View logs
docker exec -it my-service-container sh  # Access container shell if needed

5. Push Image to a Registry (for Kubernetes)

Tag the image for the registry:

docker tag my-service:1.0 <registry-url>/my-service:1.0


Push the image:

docker push <registry-url>/my-service:1.0


Kubernetes pulls this image to deploy pods.

✅ Interview Tip

You can explain it concisely in an interview like this:

“I wrote a Dockerfile specifying the base JDK image, copied the Spring Boot JAR, and set it to run on startup. I built the image with docker build, ran it locally with docker run -d -p 8080:8080, and tested that it was serving requests. Finally, I pushed the image to a container registry so Kubernetes could deploy it.”